WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:09.251
I'm thrilled to be back.

00:00:09.251 --> 00:00:11.090
This is a fantastic place.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.490
Congratulations on your new campus.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.400
So now that we can no longer say at
Harvard we have a beautiful campus to

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.325
offset our bad weather
because you've got both.

00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:21.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it's a pleasure to be here and

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.286
I did meet Peter Simp by the way.

00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:27.530
Peter has an excelent new book out called
Little Bets that he wrote on his own.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.934
Talking about the importance of how
real innovation should take place in

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:31.785
organizations.

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:38.760
And making small bets instead of betting
the farm on one large R and D program.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:45.160
But I've had, it's interesting,
I see myself as a leader but

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.380
the last ten years I haven't led anything.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.920
But I'm really excited about having
a chance to work with leaders like you.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.973
And I've decided to devote myself for
this period of my life to that.

00:00:54.973 --> 00:01:00.300
Because we've gone through what I would
call a rough patch in leadership.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.510
When I was at Medtronic,
we had the wind at our backs.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.990
That was the 90s,
when leaders were looked at as heroes and

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.290
things were going our way.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.120
Innovation was popping up all over.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.468
We did innovate some major new medical
therapies at Medtronic for heart failure.

00:01:14.468 --> 00:01:19.013
And minimally invasive cardiac surgery and
Parkinson's and Cerebral Palsy and

00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:22.410
back pain and a lot of very,
very exciting things.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:27.360
But the thing I think I'm proud of as
Medtronic is we set a metric of saying

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.230
instead of looking at our market cap,
our stock price, or revenues or profits.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.660
But how many people were
restored to full life and health,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.480
which is a mission at Medtronic.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.930
And we finally got it
down from a minute and

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.385
a half, to the time I joined the company.

00:01:39.385 --> 00:01:43.940
Until the time I left down
to one every three seconds.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.445
So today, while we're sitting here
one of every minute that goes by,

00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:50.880
every 20 people being restored
by Medtronic product.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.655
And to me that's what it's all about.

00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:53.480
Because so

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.030
many companies have lost sight of why
they went business in the first place.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.500
They lost site of their mission and
their purpose.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.580
In fact, I wrote an article recently for

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.740
fortune talking about
leadership's lost decade.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.660
Because it's been a very rough
patch I think for leadership.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.630
And this is my generation, the fact, when
I left Medtronic it was about the time

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.260
that we had the dot com decline,
but we also had Enron and WorldCom.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.040
And it wasn't just those
companies with corruption,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.080
it was the hundred companies that had to
restate billions of dollars of earnings.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.830
Because they had cooked the books and
destroyed a lot of truly great companies.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.270
Some of which are coming back,
like Xerox and Bristol Myers.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.500
But it was my generation of
leaders that led to that.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.570
And so my hope is that your generation of
leaders is going to do things differently

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.660
and not get all caught
up in playing the game.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.890
I think we also got caught up on
a lot of flawed economic theories,

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.260
the idea of shareholder
value maximization.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.420
Which morphed to short term
value maximization and

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.821
destroyed much of the pharmaceutical,
the American pharmaceutical industry.

00:02:53.821 --> 00:02:55.862
because people were playing
the short term game.

00:02:55.862 --> 00:02:58.922
And the perfect market theory and
principal agency theory, and

00:02:58.922 --> 00:03:02.107
fortunately these theories we have
now realize the flaws in them.

00:03:02.107 --> 00:03:07.130
With the meltdown in 2008, which for
you must have come as a real shock.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.380
But I think it was actually
an appropriate awakening

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.000
of what it takes to build great companies.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.700
And so
that's what I'd like to talk about today.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.500
In many, many cases,
I think the real essence of the problem

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.810
was that we got caught up
with the leader as hero.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.150
And we tried to find a rock
star to run our companies.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.290
We often went outside to people
who didn't know the company.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:30.700
Didn't know the business.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.850
And boards honestly were choosing
the wrong leaders for the wrong reasons.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.420
They were choosing people more for
their charisma than their character.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.400
More for their style than their substance.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.580
And more for
their image than their integrity.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.020
Well, if you choose people for
things like image and

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.980
style and charisma, why are you surprised
when you don't get people with integrity?

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:49.820
And that's who we had.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:54.180
We put way too many leaders put their self
interests ahead of their institution.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.421
And to me, that's the greatest sin.

00:03:55.421 --> 00:03:57.250
I've never owned my own company.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.573
I suppose if you've owned your own,
you can do what you want to.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:04.260
Although, most people own their own
company have a very long term view.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.920
But I feel like you'll always have
to put the organization first.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.163
And anyone who doesn't do that and
rises to a high position,

00:04:12.163 --> 00:04:15.613
takes advantage of their power,
has really failed as a leader.

00:04:15.613 --> 00:04:18.650
And I think, so
many of these leaders failed.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.610
So, how is your generation
going to do it different?

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.420
That's what I want to challenge you today.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.750
What kind of leader
are you going to become?

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:27.680
And how are you going to
use your leadership gifts?

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.590
So I have three questions for
you to think about.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.020
The first of all, is what kind
of leader do you want to become?

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.850
And underneath that is, where do you find
the passion to lead in the first place?

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.180
That's the first question.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.880
I just want you to reflect on this
because I'll come back to each of these.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.660
The second one, how are you going to
develop yourself as a leader?

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.980
Leadership is like anything else,
you have to practice it every day.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.260
You don't just show up in a leadership
role at age 45 and think you can lead.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.428
I had my first opportunity to lead
an organization, [INAUDIBLE] Industries.

00:04:58.428 --> 00:05:02.676
That started the consumer microwave
oven business back in 1970,

00:05:02.676 --> 00:05:04.260
when I was 27 years old.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.910
Someone bet on me at a very young age and
I made a lot of mistakes.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.405
But I tell you,
it was a fabulous experience.

00:05:09.405 --> 00:05:12.750
You learn by having those experiences.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:16.240
You don't learn just by studying other
people's experience or studying textbooks,

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.210
you learn by actually doing it.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.710
And so,
how are you going to develop yourself?

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.840
If you're going to be a great athlete, you
certainly wouldn't play in the World Cup

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.940
if you didn't practice every day or
ride in the Tour de France.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.700
Nor would you try to play the cello
at Carnegie Hall unless you

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:29.480
practiced every day.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:30.892
Well, leadership is the same thing.

00:05:30.892 --> 00:05:36.176
So, I just urge you to get in to
leadership roles as soon as possible.

00:05:36.176 --> 00:05:40.920
And constantly be processing your
leadership, I'll talk more about that.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.241
And the third question,
most important of all,

00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:45.570
how are you going to make
a difference in the world?

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.620
What do you want to do
to impact the world?

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.030
We've seen many leaders right
in this geographic area,

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.080
where people are doing it every day.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.240
How are you going to impact the world?

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.530
Rather than giving yourself over to
somebody else telling you what to do,

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.200
how are you going to make that impact?

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.710
Because we desperately need
contributions of leaders.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:11.980
The complex problems we have today like
global peace, global health, education,

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:17.242
the environment, energy policies,
healthcare cannot be solved.

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:24.610
It cannot be solved by any one company,
one individual, any one sector.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.110
We really need people, collaborative
leaders who can bring people together,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.360
to really make a difference in the world.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.240
So that's the challenge I
want to give you today.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.220
So let me talk about how I think
leadership is going to be different

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:38.680
in your generation.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.120
Because I think what I grew up with
was a hierarchical view of leadership,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.520
I didn't have that view but
that's what was visited on us.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.420
It came out of the two World Wars,

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:50.240
the Great Depression, we thought
General Patton was some kind of hero.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.240
The all powerful person on top,
we venerated those leaders.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.110
And I think today, as a result,

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:02.090
a lot of leaders created hierarchy,
bureaucracy, rules and regulations.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.460
You don't want to work in
those kind of organizations.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.670
I don't know any young leaders
today that want to work in that,

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.450
in fact most have rejected that notion.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.280
And I think that's a good thing.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.580
You had knowledge workers working for you.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.050
And I used to say to people,
middle managers, if the people working for

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.010
you don't know more than you do,
then you've got the wrong people.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:21.290
You need to get some new people.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:26.830
Because you want to have more talented
people around you than you are,

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.550
in every single position.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:34.010
But I think the most important thing is,
in organizations today,

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.570
we fooled ourselves by saying,
people are only interested in money.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:40.310
That may be true in some sectors,
that may be true at the top.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:45.291
But I can tell you, served on the board
of Target stores, 350,000 people.

00:07:45.291 --> 00:07:47.396
People make a dollar or
two over minimum wage.

00:07:47.396 --> 00:07:51.038
I can guarantee you, it isn’t just
about money, it’s about meaning,

00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:53.200
it’s about the purpose.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:53.921
Think about your work.

00:07:53.921 --> 00:07:56.670
You’re going to spend more time at work
than you do anything else in your life.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.920
More time with your families,
more time with your leisure time,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.525
probably more time you will sleeping.

00:08:01.525 --> 00:08:03.730
Don't you have the right to
have a meeting at your work?

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:05.680
Doesn't everyone who works
in the organization?

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.350
People are not just gnomes or
cogs in a wheel.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.310
You need to think about how
do you provide that meeting?

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:15.350
At Medtronic, the most significant
thing we did is every December,

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.390
we got all of our employees together.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:21.450
Minneapolis employees, as many you could
get into a large atrium auditorium.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:25.291
Not fancy like this, just a big
atrium area about 3, 4,000 people and

00:08:25.291 --> 00:08:30.680
these days 25 or 30,000 people watching
around the world and on web streaming.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.020
And we brought in six patients.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.410
And these six patients told their story

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.960
of what it had meant to
have a Medtronic product.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.650
The doctors came with them and introduced
them, but they didn't tell the story.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.120
And it was a very impactful experience.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:43.535
I'll never forget the first one I went to.

00:08:43.535 --> 00:08:46.590
5 people came up,
1 of them was a man in his 50s.

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.230
Who had intractable back pain and
he failed five surgeries.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:55.850
And he actually said, I was an alcoholic,
drug addicted, anything to stop the pain.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:58.677
And then, he said,
then I got your stimulator, and

00:08:58.677 --> 00:09:00.360
he actually broke into tears.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:01.830
He said, it took my pain away.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:04.080
I just realized back last March, for

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.350
the first time what it
was like to be pain free.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:08.390
First time in ten years.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:11.410
Then, a young boy came up who
is the same age as my son Jeff,

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.630
18 years old from Pittsburgh,
but there the similarities end.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.690
He described it like he had
cerebral palsy from birth.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.480
Every year he had surgery.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.750
Finally, at 16, he denied future surgery.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.580
His body got very rigid and stiff,

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:28.490
his motions quite spastic,
he had to pull out of a mainstream school.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:32.285
And then, he smiled and he patted his
belly and said, then I got your pump,

00:09:32.285 --> 00:09:33.563
it's my friendly ally.

00:09:33.563 --> 00:09:38.930
Which put an older drug directly into
his spinal column, and it restored him.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.940
It didn't take away his cerebral palsy,
but it gave him life.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.810
And he said, now I'm back in mainstream
school, I got rid of the wheelchair,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.630
I can walk in arm braces.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.876
His speech patterns are corrected,
I'm going to college next year,

00:09:50.876 --> 00:09:51.764
got a girlfriend.

00:09:51.764 --> 00:09:57.170
He wasn't correct,
he wasn't cured but he had a life.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.640
And that's when I got what
Medtronic really does,

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.060
it was about the mission of the company.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.690
And that mission can apply to
any organization you work in,

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.460
it can apply to financial services
organization, it can apply to a consulting

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:11.020
firm, certainly it can apply with all
the high tech companies around here.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:18.300
So I'd like to describe 21st century
leadership in 4 words, very simply.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:21.265
Align, empower, serve, and collaborate.

00:10:21.265 --> 00:10:24.510
Let me describe briefly what
I mean by each of those.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.990
The toughest job a leader is
not getting the numbers right.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.130
I dare say any half dozen
of us could go off and

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:33.050
figure out how to get the numbers right
for just about any company in the world.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:37.452
All you have to do is cut 20% of the
staff, reduce someone else's salary 10%,

00:10:37.452 --> 00:10:40.940
get rid of bonuses, cut the R&amp;D 30%,
cut the marketing 20.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:44.150
And I'll guarantee you
the stock price will go up.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.417
That's what's happening at
Pfizer right now, real time.

00:10:47.417 --> 00:10:51.280
But you want to have a company,
if you do that.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.400
The toughest job of leadership is getting
people aligned around the mission and

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:56.200
values of the company.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.320
And how else can it be?

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.400
Think about it.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:10:59.590
What's the hardest job we have?

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:00.630
Running a global company.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.110
You got people all around the world
that are raised in different cultures.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:07.550
You're expanding quickly in Brazil,
or in China, or in India.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.930
And you have to convince
people that your mission and

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:13.060
your values are really essential
to the future of the company.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:15.590
You can't run multiple
value systems in a company.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:19.610
You can have different norms but
you have to have one value set.

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.100
So that people can work collaboratively,
all around the globe.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:27.870
The second thing, is leadership is not
about exerting power over other people.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:29.182
That's a very old fashioned notion.

00:11:29.182 --> 00:11:32.020
The most popular course at Harvard
used to be power and influence,

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.330
now no one takes it.

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:37.170
It's about having the capacity to empower
other people to step up and lead.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:41.351
And I'll guarantee you, an organization
of empowered people from top to bottom

00:11:41.351 --> 00:11:45.430
will out-compete a tops down
power-based organization every time.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.110
Think about where you want to work.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.520
And so,
are you that kind of empowering leader?

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:52.350
I remember visiting a company,
a Medtronic company,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.650
out in Southern California in Irvine.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:55.548
They make heart valves.

00:11:55.548 --> 00:11:59.390
They take pigs' hearts and about one out
of every ten pigs' hearts produce a valve

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:04.280
that can be used to replace a failed
human aortic or mitral valve.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:05.472
And this was heavily backlogged.

00:12:05.472 --> 00:12:07.430
Took 18 months to train new workers.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:12.110
A lot of newly immigrated
immigrants from Southeast Asia, and

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.510
I met the top worker in the facility,
a woman named Trin.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:15.830
She was from Laos.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.400
And so,
she had all her tools on the bench.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.810
I said Trin, I'm trained as an industrial
engineer, tell me how you do your work

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.830
because we need to accelerate this,
make it more of a science than an art.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:24.890
She wouldn't look down at her bench.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.790
She said, Mr. George,
let me tell you something.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.870
My job is to make heart valves
that save someone's life, and

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.860
if I do that job well,
a lot of people can be restored.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.876
She said, I make 1,000 valves a year,
I make the quality decisions here.

00:12:36.876 --> 00:12:41.635
We've got a manual, but my criteria,
is that valve good enough for

00:12:41.635 --> 00:12:45.280
my father, my husband, or my daughter?

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.650
And if it's not, it doesn't get by me.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:53.330
She said, for you,
99.9% quality is considered very good.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:56.210
But if one of the valves I make fails,
someone's going to die, and

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:57.410
I could never live with that.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.340
So every one of those 1,000
valves has got to be perfect.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.147
But she said, you know what I think,
Will, when I go home at night?

00:13:02.147 --> 00:13:07.082
I'm thinking about those 5,000 people who
are alive and well in the world today,

00:13:07.082 --> 00:13:09.790
because of the heart valves I made.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.460
Now, this woman has no direct reports.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.840
Do any of you doubt that
she's an empowered person?

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:15.560
She's actually an empowered leader,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.910
because she's the one that
people go to for quality advice.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:21.380
She's the one that does
the training in the facility.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:25.770
So leadership today is about having people
like Trin throughout your organizations,

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:27.990
to empower other people to step up and
lead.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.790
That's the only way we're going to
have great organizations.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.550
The third thing is,
leadership is about service.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:39.360
It's not to serve the shareholder,
it's to serve the people, the customers,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.900
the patients, the clients that you serve,
that's what it's about.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.380
It's not serving the shareholder, and

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:47.620
if you don't get that, your company
will never create shareholder value.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:52.290
If you get that, if you create better
products, and any company today that can't

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:56.940
make a product that offers better value,
better technology, better innovation, or

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.880
better services than their competitors
eventually go out of business.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.650
Look at General Motors,
it took 50 years to go out of business.

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.070
But it was sure as you can stand here,

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.190
they had to go out of business
before they could get it right.

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:10.960
Because they didn't understand
whom they were serving.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:14.290
The fourth thing is,
leadership is about collaboration.

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:18.230
And the new model is,
how do we have a collaborative model?

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:22.970
Sam Pomasano is one of the pioneers
that IBM did this about seven years ago.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:28.350
And he changed IBM away from a hierarchy
model into a fully collaborative model.

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.440
And he's totally transformed that company.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.190
And they've done extremely
well in that time period.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.050
Because it's about global collaboration.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:38.240
They get compensated on what
kind of collaborators they are.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.720
So I think the great organizations and
the great leaders,

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:45.120
of which I hope you'll be one, understand
how to align people around mission values,

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:47.490
how to empower other people to step up and
lead.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:53.600
Recognize that leadership is about serving
your customers and serving your employees.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:55.614
And finally, it's about collaboration.

00:14:55.614 --> 00:14:59.887
And I think in that is the essence of the
kind of leaders I hope you'll become and

00:14:59.887 --> 00:15:02.080
will think seriously about becoming.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.990
I wrote a book as was mentioned,
True North, with Peter Sims.

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:11.120
We interviewed 125 leaders,
many of them from the West Coast.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:13.618
And my colleague said, Bill,
I hope you'll finally crack the code.

00:15:13.618 --> 00:15:16.120
Are great leaders made or born?

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:17.943
What are the characteristics
of successful leaders?

00:15:17.943 --> 00:15:21.249
You know what, 123 people we talked to,
no one wanted to talk about that.

00:15:21.249 --> 00:15:23.048
You know what they want to talk about?

00:15:23.048 --> 00:15:24.488
They wanted to talk about who they were.

00:15:24.488 --> 00:15:27.180
They wanted to talk about
their life stories.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:28.510
Where their passions came from,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:32.540
and they wanted to talk about really
difficult times that they'd face.

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:35.960
I remember talking to Dick Kirachewitz
who was a graduate of this school,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:38.840
the most successful commercial banker for
20 years.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:41.550
And Dick was saying, I didn't learn
leadership from Stanford Business,

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.620
I learned it from a little
town in western Washington.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:47.730
In a sawmill town, he said, I learned
two things that were really important.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.161
I learned it at the corner grocery
store and on the athletic field.

00:15:50.161 --> 00:15:52.680
He was a very good quarterback,
by the way.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:55.580
But he said, if you had 11 quarterbacks
on the team, you'd lose every game.

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:58.520
He said, at Wells Fargo, everyone that
worked for me had to be better than I was.

00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:02.200
And the second thing he said,
I learned At the corner grocery store

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:05.490
it was all about the last 3 feet
between you and the customer.

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.570
We've lost site of that in healthcare
the last 3 feet between the doctor and

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:09.720
the patient.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:13.850
That's where the relationship in
every organization takes place.

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.390
And if you don't get that, you probably
won't create a great organization.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:21.688
And you probably won't be able to
sustain success over the long term.

00:16:21.688 --> 00:16:26.579
But about 85% of the people told us that
they've experienced very severe crucibles

00:16:26.579 --> 00:16:27.457
in their life.

00:16:27.457 --> 00:16:31.330
One of them was Dan Vasella,
who I got to know well, a CEO of Novartis.

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:36.520
Dan was born as a sickly boy, and
not born, but was sickly as a child.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.950
He had spinal meningitis and tuberculosis,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.670
which in the 1960s were very
life threatening diseases.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:49.210
He went into a hospital and then
a sanitorium and he spent 12 months there.

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:53.240
His parents who lived nearby with
good public transportation never

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:54.580
visited him once.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:55.980
And he was scared.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.580
He said that every week they would
come in and do a spinal tap.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:16:59.910
He thought that they
were trying to kill him.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:02.797
No one told him what was going on and
he would scream.

00:17:02.797 --> 00:17:04.996
And then he said,
a new physician came in and

00:17:04.996 --> 00:17:08.682
he explained this whole procedure to
me and walked me through every step,

00:17:08.682 --> 00:17:12.486
treated me like an adult and he said,
can I help you make this less painful and

00:17:12.486 --> 00:17:16.270
he said, yes, don't hold me down
like an animal like you were.

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:20.910
Just hold my hand and he did and he said,
it didn't hurt, so I gave him a big hug.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.420
But Dan's problems didn't end there.

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:23.939
He had one sister die of cancer,

00:17:23.939 --> 00:17:27.785
another sister he was very close to died
in an automobile crash when Dan was 12.

00:17:27.785 --> 00:17:31.223
When he was 13,
his father died in heart surgery, and

00:17:31.223 --> 00:17:36.290
his mother went to work in a distant town
about, and came back once every 3 weeks.

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:40.362
And here this 14 year old boy was left
home alone, he joined a motorcycle gang.

00:17:40.362 --> 00:17:42.880
He got a lot of drinking,
a lot of fighting.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:44.390
Finally, at 18 he came out of this,

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:48.520
he said,
I want to make something out of my life.

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.930
I want to be like that compassionate
physician, that's why he went to medical

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:56.140
school and eventually when he became Chief
Resident at the University of Basel and

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:59.230
decided, I think that I can serve
more than one person at a time.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:01.540
And that's when he went to work
in the pharmaceutical business.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:03.940
Now, I can guarantee you, of all
the people in the pharmaceutical business,

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:07.600
he's the one that has the most compassion
for patients, and is really deeply

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:12.480
concerned about these issues because
of that Syrian crucible experience.

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:15.730
I wrote him about a friend of
our son who's having a severe

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.420
healthcare issue right now, Dan wrote
him back, never even met the young man,

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:22.560
probably never will on a Sunday afternoon,
took time to explain the entire disease.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:26.240
That's the kind of compassion that
we need to have in leaders today but

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.300
see it came out of that
crucible that he had.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:32.740
Now, I don't have a dramatic
story to tell you like Steve Jobs

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:34.580
did in his commencement address here.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.860
Or like Howard Schultz does when
he wrote about in True North.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:40.470
But I came from a middle to
upper middle class family.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.930
My father, I thought,
was a good consultant.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:43.944
He thought he was a failure.

00:18:43.944 --> 00:18:47.910
I'm the only child of older parents,
he was 43 when I was born.

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:51.960
He said, son, I'd like you to
become the leader I never became.

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:53.280
And so, here, I'm an eight year old boy.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.710
He's already telling me, son, you could
be president of the Coca-Cola company.

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:59.630
He's even naming the company and
he said, if that doesn't work,

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:01.460
you can go to work at Procter &amp; Gamble.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:02.400
That's a really great company.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:03.290
Be head of that or

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:06.600
you could work at this new computer
company on the East Coast called IBM.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.900
Well, of course, I didn't know what these
companies were and I drank Coca-Cola, but

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.160
I could tell you it was pretty heavy
tip to lay on an eight year old kids.

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:18.679
But lo and behold, I'm in high school,
junior high joined the lots of

00:19:18.679 --> 00:19:21.947
organizations and
I never selected to lead anything.

00:19:21.947 --> 00:19:25.533
I am never head of an organization,
never elected student council.

00:19:25.533 --> 00:19:29.158
I'm a good tennis player, never elected
captain of my tennis team, finally,

00:19:29.158 --> 00:19:31.860
my senior year of high school I
said to heck with it, I ran for

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.620
president senior class
which I nominated myself.

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:38.270
Guess one other guy, and I was sure
I was a better leader than he was.

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:42.043
When the votes came in,
I lost by a margin of two to one.

00:19:42.043 --> 00:19:45.034
So you can see the kids in my high
school didn't appreciate what a great

00:19:45.034 --> 00:19:45.675
leader I was.

00:19:45.675 --> 00:19:49.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So to escape myself, I used to say,

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:51.500
I went to Georgia Tech
because of great weather.

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:55.140
I wanted to play tennis, a great
academic school to learn engineering.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.740
But the real reason was I wanted to
go somewhere where no one knew me.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:02.550
But I hadn't learned at that point
is wherever you go, there you are.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:06.230
You may change venues, but
you take yourself with you and I did.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:12.310
And I was eager to get ahead, and so
I was going to lead everyone, so I ran for

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:18.080
election six more times at Georgia Tech,
lost all six, so now I'm zero for seven.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:20.640
So it's pretty clear no-one
sees me as a leader.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:23.320
The very best thing that happened to
me is some seniors at Georgia Tech

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.500
took me aside and said, Bill,
no one's ever going to work with you,

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:30.680
much less be led by you, because you're so
ambitious to get ahead, you're moving so

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:33.910
fast, you know take time for other people.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:37.155
Don't take time, I'll tell you that so
I can blow it to the Solar Plexis.

00:20:37.155 --> 00:20:40.360
I thought, I'm never going to
be the leader I want to become.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:44.260
And so, I took their advice and
put my own self-help,

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:47.700
leadership program together,
talked to a lot of people who rejected me.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.841
And eventually had another leadership
position to the college and

00:20:50.841 --> 00:20:53.597
graduate school, and
I went to Harvard Business School.

00:20:53.597 --> 00:20:56.420
But I came out of school and
started the year to get ahead.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:21:01.510
I joined Layton industries, thinking,
I can move ahead quickly in this company,

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.880
spent ten years there,
then I joined Honeywell.

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:10.610
And seeing here's an opportunity to
run ultimately a great global company.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:15.020
And I had the privilege of being president
of Honeywell Europe in my late 30s.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:17.730
Came back and
I was given a series of turnarounds.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:21.030
And I've always thought of myself
as a builder, a grower of things.

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:24.400
I know how to turn businesses around but
that's not where my heart is.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.740
But we got the first set of turnarounds
done, it was like a huge bureaucracy.

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:30.140
It was a two step promotion,
worst promotion I ever got.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:32.870
Be careful about your
promotions because this one had

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:36.380
I was a sector head with three groups,
nine divisions, different business.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.030
I've always loved being engaged
with customers and employees.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:42.390
Well, we were chasing numbers and
there are huge turnarounds.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:45.900
I'd spend all of my time doing that, of
course, with laying off a lot of people.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:47.530
And so, I got that one done.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.650
They gave me a second set of turnarounds.

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:51.784
And I got that one
happened pretty quickly.

00:21:51.784 --> 00:21:53.280
Then, the third set came along.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.970
Aerospace and Defense business
which important business, but

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:56.935
not where my heart is.

00:21:56.935 --> 00:21:59.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so,

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.600
found 500 million in
defense contract overruns.

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:05.180
Had to write that off,
which in those days was real money.

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:06.850
And then, one day I'm coming home.

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:10.690
It's a day like today in the fall,
Minnesota, where the leaves are turning.

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:13.350
By this time, Penny and
I had been married 16, 17 years.

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:16.180
She has a good job as
a consulting psychologist.

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:19.550
Our sons Jeff and
John are going into high school.

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:21.580
We have a lot of friends in Minnesota.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:24.840
I'm one of the two leading candidates,
not the leading candidate,

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:29.160
to be the next CEO of Honeywell when they
make the decision in four or five years.

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:32.680
I look at myself in the mirror and
I'm miserable.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:35.610
You ever had that flash in the mirror,
you see yourself as you really are.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:39.300
Not like narcissistic looking in this
pool saying, how beautiful I am.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.980
But what's happening is I've always
seen myself as a valued senator leader

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:44.000
that I was losing it.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:45.970
That I was playing the corporate game.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.720
That I was even wearing
cufflinks which I don't wear.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.670
Nothing wrong with cufflinks but
I was trying to impress everyone.

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:55.910
Say, just the right thing at the right
time, not showing any passion because I

00:22:55.910 --> 00:23:03.100
knew I was a passion free company, and
just kind of be buttoned up, be the man.

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:04.560
Well, I went home and told Penny that.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:05.340
You know what she said?

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.260
She said, Bill, I’ve been trying
to tell you that for a year.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:10.110
You just refuse to listen.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:14.209
See, it’s always the person closest to
you that sees you as you really are.

00:23:14.209 --> 00:23:17.330
And she saw through my blind
spots which I had plenty of.

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.160
I don’t have any blind spots,
so I had a lot of blind spots.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.276
So I have a men’s group, I went to
them the next day, and they said,

00:23:23.276 --> 00:23:27.076
you've turned my [INAUDIBLE] down three
times to become president of the company.

00:23:27.076 --> 00:23:28.592
Why?
Rick said, well,

00:23:28.592 --> 00:23:32.375
it's not a really big company,
it's a midsize company.

00:23:32.375 --> 00:23:33.750
But I did get up the courage.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:37.170
I called Earl Bakken the founder,
because I had to turn it out by four or

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.100
five months before.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:42.200
I say, is the job being president chief
[INAUDIBLE] number two job still open?

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:44.700
And it was,
I remember spending three hours with Earl.

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:45.700
He didn't even interview me.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:49.010
All he did was talk about
the mission of the company.

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:50.070
I did take the job.

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:51.810
I remember walking in
the door as I first did.

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:53.250
Never been in the building.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:54.930
I felt like I was coming home.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:55.880
What an odd feeling.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.820
Coming home to a place
you've never been before.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:23:59.320
You ever had that feeling?

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.950
Where you're coming in and you say,
here's good people I can work with.

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:06.150
I can learn a lot from them and
we can really make a difference and

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:10.870
I can tell you honestly guys, it was
the best decision of my professional life.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:16.490
Had the best 13 years there and
everything has happened since that time

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:21.540
back in 1989 has opened up because of that
and it was a wonderful time in my life.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.396
But if I had not gone through
that searing crucible,

00:24:24.396 --> 00:24:27.720
that difficult time all the way back
to high school, and I hope you can see

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:32.180
the linkage here of kind of repeating
some of those same mistakes.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:36.000
But becoming real and becoming authentic
and finding I could be myself and

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.660
be successful and
not the guy that wore cuff links but

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:42.810
the person that could just be real
really was a fantastic thing.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:46.710
So let's talk about how are you
going to develop as a leader.

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:50.620
because I think that's really important,
I think you want to start with

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:55.460
your life story, your difficult times, and
where do you find your passion to lead?

00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:58.410
And it's having that self awareness.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:02.260
We've had these myths that the smartest
person makes the best leader,

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:04.000
it is indeed a myth.

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.580
Anyone with an IQ over 120,
which all of you have.

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:13.210
The differentiating factor in leaders is
not IQ but EQ, so emotional intelligence.

00:25:13.210 --> 00:25:17.260
Dan Goldman's written about that, they
have the research data showing this now.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:19.090
That that's the differentiating factor.

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:22.890
And it starts with your self awareness,
knowing who you are.

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:26.010
I teach some at and John Rice,
the vice chairman comes and

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:27.300
he says, you know you want to get ahead.

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:30.970
These are all the hard charges and chiefs,
and you want to get here, ahead here.

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:33.770
You need to let management
know who you are.

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:36.040
He said, no I don't think
you understood what I meant.

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:37.640
You need to let them know
who you are as a person.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:38.940
Not what you want them to think you are.

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:43.140
Not trying to impress them,
not what we want you to be but

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:46.360
who are you and
if you can lead for that solid,

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:51.810
authentic core, you will have not just a
better life you'll be a far better leader.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.230
But to do that you have to have
a level of self awareness and

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:55.710
that's easier said than done.

00:25:55.710 --> 00:26:00.220
The oracle Delphi told us 4,000 years
ago know thyself, how do you do that?

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.170
Well I think there's no substitute for
experience.

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:05.130
That's why I say the first
thing get experience early.

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:09.690
Best leaders in my classroom
are military vets, you know?

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:13.960
And they come someone like Morris Sullivan
who is running logistic missions for

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:18.130
the Marines at age 22 and
23 and had to go to Fallujah.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.370
Actually got the real experience,
but you got to have the experience.

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:22.910
But then it's not just enough to have it.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.950
GE gives people a lot of experience,
but they never stop the process.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:26.930
They're moving so fast,

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:30.110
they keep repeating their mistakes
in every job they move into.

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:32.530
So what you want to do,
is you have to process.

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:37.290
You need some form of reflective or
introspective process.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:38.480
So think about, what do you do?

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:40.590
I happen to meditate 20
minutes twice a day.

00:26:40.590 --> 00:26:41.650
There are many things you can do.

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:43.960
You can go for a jog,
you can take a long walk,

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.160
you can have someone close to you,
like my wife, Penny, that you can talk to.

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:50.160
You can pray.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:54.250
But you need to have some way
of processing in your life,

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:55.670
what's going right, what's going wrong.

00:26:55.670 --> 00:26:57.610
Steve Jobs had a wonderful statement.

00:26:57.610 --> 00:26:59.160
He said, I look in the mirror
every morning, and say,

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:02.490
Steve are you doing today
exactly what you want to do?

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:06.300
The answer's not going to be yes everyday,
but if it's no too many days in a row,

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:10.030
you need to change what you're doing and
do what you want to do.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:13.520
As I always say to the MBA's,
never sell your soul to the man, you know.

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.860
You gotta be your person.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:19.440
And I think the third thing is,
have people,

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:22.140
a group of people around you I think

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:25.540
you heard that the leadership perspectives
course here started with small groups.

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.770
I got that idea from a group of MBAs,
two from here, three from Harvard,

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:29.380
one from Duke.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:31.840
I asked him how do you want to
develop yourselves as leaders?

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:35.370
And we decided that small groups, and this
was very threatening to the HPS faculty

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:40.140
I can tell you, could be more impactful
than having faculty members participating.

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:45.108
So we now have the course where we've
had 15 hundred people go through.

00:27:45.108 --> 00:27:50.040
And small groups, six-person
groups including 400 executives.

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:53.570
We even do it in a week
in exec-ed programs.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:55.442
This came out of a men's group I've had.

00:27:55.442 --> 00:27:57.630
Those are the people I want to talk to,

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:02.320
about you know I've had meets every
Wednesday morning from 7:15 to 8:30.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.972
After 36 years, let me tell you something,

00:28:04.972 --> 00:28:09.120
the most important things I've ever
done to develop as a leaders because

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:13.020
these people know me inside-out
where I was coming from.

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.710
A few years ago my wife
Penny had breast cancer.

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:18.470
I went to talk to them because Penny and
I were having a little dance.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.500
I was the rational person who
was going to solve the problem.

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:23.240
You know cancer is the one
problem you can't solve.

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:26.800
And so
she thought she was going to die and

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:29.470
I was saying, no you'll be fine and
here's the data.

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:31.710
And in fact I presented this data and
she would say,

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:34.320
don't give me your damned
data with me you know it says

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:39.130
it's 80% chance to be non-recurrent
said for me it's a hundred or zero.

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:41.310
But I went to my men's group and
they said Bill,

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:46.120
you're in denial because remember
that your mother died of cancer your

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:50.220
fiance died 18 months later three
weeks to the day before the wedding.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:52.290
You just can't think
that penny might be gone.

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:55.970
They were right, I had that blind spot and
I couldn't see that.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:57.370
So who's that group in your life?

00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:59.030
Who is that group of really close people?

00:28:59.030 --> 00:29:00.030
Who would you go to?

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:02.260
I guarantee you,
I use Facebook and Twitter a lot,

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:04.980
I'll guarantee you you're not going to
put it on your Twitter account

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:09.900
that you just got fired from your job or
had a life threatening illness.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.730
Or having difficulties in your marriage.

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:13.000
But who would you talk to?

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:13.690
Who are those people?

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:15.610
Because I think you need
those in leadership.

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:19.160
We had 36 CEOs come back to Harvard for
a reunion of our CEO program, and

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:20.960
we talked to them all individually.

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:22.780
You know what they said
the biggest threat,

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.050
the most significant problem they had?

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:29.270
Loneliness, leadership is lonely
unless you have people around you.

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:31.260
That's why I wrote the new book,
True North Groups,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:35.320
to talk about how you can have a group in
your life, someone you can really talk to.

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:39.290
I tell you it's the best way to
develop leaders that I know of.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:42.810
Much better than all these programs out
there that are trying to cultivate you to

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:46.300
the GE, or the McKenzie, or
the Proctor and Gamma model.

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:50.550
Because it allows you to be authentic and
be who you are and people around

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:54.850
you can help you stay on track and
stay true to what you believe in.

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:57.860
The other thing I think is
important to your leadership

00:29:57.860 --> 00:29:59.380
is that you have an integrated life.

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:04.130
I remember back in my 30's I had anything
but that, I was flying all over the world,

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.430
I had two kids at home, plenty working.

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:11.440
You know and, you know I was one person
at home and a different person at work.

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:13.660
And I finally went to an offsite seminar,

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:16.140
I said you gotta take all
these walls down in your life,

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:20.795
decompartmentalize your life, and
just be the same person everywhere.

00:30:20.795 --> 00:30:22.560
You're never going to have
perfect balance in your life, but

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:25.610
you can be, maintain the integrity
of being the same person, and

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:29.240
not trying to pretend you're
something different than you are.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:31.380
So think about your
leadership development.

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:33.430
How are you going to
develop your leadership?

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:34.980
What kind of leader do you want to become?

00:30:36.050 --> 00:30:37.890
And then we get to the third question,
okay?

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.090
And that's the most
important question of all.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:43.980
How are you going to use your leadership
gifts to make a difference in the world?

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.720
At the end of the day, what are you
going to say that's really important?

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:48.320
What's the purpose of your leadership?

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:52.170
I hope it goes beyond just making
a lot of money for yourself.

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:55.890
Yes, you'll pay off your debts,
I guarantee you will.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:31:00.290
I hope it goes beyond just trying to
achieve a title or something like that.

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:04.090
I hope it will really be something
that you can leave that makes

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:06.990
the difference in the world because you
have that ability, that's why you're here.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.590
That's why you're chosen to come here.

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:10.000
Look at all people that
come before you and

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.990
the difference they've made in the world.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:14.360
Look at this gift from this Bob King,

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:17.710
I was just given a gift here to
study poverty and entrepreneurship.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:20.800
Think about what people can do to
make a difference in the world, and

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:22.370
what are you going to do?

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:24.770
Think about that,
because I really want to challenge you.

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:26.740
And I want you to think about it this way.

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:29.090
Okay, you're at the end of your life.

00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:30.846
Okay, you probably don't
want to think about that.

00:31:30.846 --> 00:31:34.550
The good news is you're 97 years old,
so you've led a long life.

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:36.490
Bad news you've got three hours to live,
and

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:38.990
all your adult children
are gathered around.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:40.200
All of your grandchildren have come in.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:45.340
I am thinking of my favorite granddaughter
Dillon who is four years old, and

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:48.260
she looks up at you and says Grandpa or

00:31:48.260 --> 00:31:50.700
Grandma what did you do to make
a difference in the world?

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:51.930
What are you going to tell her?

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:54.650
I hope you don't tell her you made so

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:57.145
much money she never has to work
a day in her life like you did.

00:31:57.145 --> 00:31:58.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:00.980
I hope you will tell her what's
really important in life because

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:02.700
that's the legacy you're leaving.

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:03.860
But don't wait until then.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:05.970
You leave that legacy every day.

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:09.020
Robert F Kennedy once
said when he was alive,

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:11.550
few will have the greatness
to bend history itself.

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:15.320
He happened to be in South Africa where
Nelson Mandela did indeed bend history.

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:17.710
But most of us never will, I know I won't.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:21.400
But he went on to say but each of us can
commit ourselves to a series of actions to

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.200
make this world a better place.

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:27.828
And the sum total of all those actions
will write the history of this generation.

00:32:27.828 --> 00:32:31.687
Well, it's my hope that your generation
will really make a difference and

00:32:31.687 --> 00:32:33.730
lead better in a different way.

00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:37.300
A more authentic way, a more sustainable
way and create great companies.

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:41.270
My colleague Michael Porter
wrote an article

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:44.830
last January in Harvard Business Review
called Creating Shared Value.

00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:48.530
What's the value for society your
organization's going to create?

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:52.580
We can think of lots of examples of people
that do that, organizations that do that.

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:56.150
But how are you going to make
a difference in your leadership?

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:57.990
Margaret Mead I think said that well,

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:02.040
never doubt the power of a small
group of people to change the world.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:05.461
Indeed, it is the only
thing that ever has.

00:33:05.461 --> 00:33:09.503
I'd like to close by saying when you leave
this world, the only thing you can take

00:33:09.503 --> 00:33:13.366
with you is what you'll leave behind that
I want to challenge you today to say,

00:33:13.366 --> 00:33:16.948
what do you going to leave behind that
will make this world a better place.

00:33:16.948 --> 00:33:18.526
Thank you very much.

00:33:18.526 --> 00:33:28.526
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:33:35.302 --> 00:33:37.172
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's open up for questions.

00:33:37.172 --> 00:33:40.389
To whoever gets to ask the first question,
got a book to sign for you.

00:33:40.389 --> 00:33:45.848
[LAUGH] Who would like to
ask the first question?

00:33:45.848 --> 00:33:46.700
Thank you.

00:33:50.391 --> 00:33:52.200
&gt;&gt; Hi, thanks for that amazing talk.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:55.100
When you're running a company
like Medtronic, which is so

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:58.680
devoted to human health but
also committed to shareholder value,

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:02.560
how do you balance those two when
they might come into conflict?

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:06.610
And I'm thinking, for example,
of the JBJS issue that was

00:34:06.610 --> 00:34:10.668
earlier this year about Journal
of Bone and Joint Surgery.

00:34:10.668 --> 00:34:13.253
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; It was all about spine surgery being

00:34:13.253 --> 00:34:15.210
sort of questionable value.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.240
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And Medtronic's role in spine surgery.

00:34:17.240 --> 00:34:19.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
what my successor's successor,

00:34:19.930 --> 00:34:23.182
Omar Ishrak did is he set up
a whole study to study Yale,

00:34:23.182 --> 00:34:28.345
which is known as a medical school that's
not very favorable towards industry.

00:34:28.345 --> 00:34:30.795
But yeah,
I think bad things are going to happen.

00:34:30.795 --> 00:34:34.145
You cannot run a company of any magnitude
and not have bad things happen and

00:34:34.145 --> 00:34:35.465
have challenging issues.

00:34:35.465 --> 00:34:38.005
And I think the real issue is,
how do you deal with it?

00:34:38.005 --> 00:34:40.340
When it comes to you like
this Penn State fiasco.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:43.920
Bad things happen in organizations,
but how do you deal with it?

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:46.230
Do you step up to it and
say we're going to do the right thing.

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:48.040
We're going to pull that
product off the market,

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:49.120
we're going to study it objectively,

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:52.320
we're going to fund whatever it
takes to get the root cause of that.

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:55.610
You don't go into denial like
Merck originally did it in Vioxx.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:59.182
You've got to deal with the problem and
say, what's the right thing to do?

00:34:59.182 --> 00:35:03.948
And I think, yes, and I'll tell you the
best way to destroy shareholder value is

00:35:03.948 --> 00:35:08.425
to have a quality problem or
have an organization that's in denial.

00:35:08.425 --> 00:35:09.880
because you may get through
the short-term, but

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:11.640
you won't get through the long-term.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:14.340
We do have to make the numbers.

00:35:14.340 --> 00:35:16.250
We had to make the numbers at Medtronic.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:19.600
But what you always want to do is make
sure you're investing in the long term.

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:23.434
If you make the numbers by sacrificing the
long term to make the numbers look good in

00:35:23.434 --> 00:35:25.940
a short term,
that will come back to bite you and

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:27.730
you won't have a company in the long run.

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:29.850
That's the General Motors story,
the Sears Roebuck story.

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:31.750
I can give you lots of other examples,

00:35:31.750 --> 00:35:35.170
more recent ones that are real tragedies,
okay?

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:39.160
We've seen one right next here,
my role model company for 30 years.

00:35:39.160 --> 00:35:41.010
You know what it was?

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:42.020
Hewlett Packard.

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:44.180
I met Dave Packard once,
I'll tell you he and

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:45.630
Bill Hewlett knew how to run a company.

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:47.960
Very egalitarian, everyone matters.

00:35:49.050 --> 00:35:50.240
You could go up and talk to him anytime.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.560
They're wandering around the labs.

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:55.110
Then somehow with 300,000 people they

00:35:55.110 --> 00:35:56.930
weren't able to find any
leaders from within.

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.920
Don't ask me how you can take
an organization with 300,000 people and

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:01.570
not find one person to lead it.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:03.890
So they went outside, and
then they did it again, and

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:08.120
again and again with a quite
dysfunctional board of directors.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:09.210
And I'm not trying to rip on them.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:13.330
I'm just saying if you don't keep focused
on those long term issues, if you abandon

00:36:13.330 --> 00:36:17.490
what got you there, the HP way, and
you don't stay true to your values,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.590
if Metronic ever abandons the idea of
restoring people to full life and health.

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:23.640
But, honestly, that's where real
shareholder value is created.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:26.230
The only reason we create all that
shareholder value is because we did good

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:28.870
value for patients and
we could charge fairly for

00:36:28.870 --> 00:36:31.420
it, and people got excited about it.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:33.187
Employees don't get motivated
just by trying to get stock.

00:36:33.187 --> 00:36:36.251
At least I found they didn't get
motivated by trying the stock price up.

00:36:36.251 --> 00:36:38.111
So, thank you for that question.

00:36:38.111 --> 00:36:40.075
Other questions, yes sir.

00:36:40.075 --> 00:36:41.655
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for your talk.

00:36:41.655 --> 00:36:45.811
The concept of authentic leadership that
he mentioned really doesn't with me, and

00:36:45.811 --> 00:36:48.742
I was wondering if you have thoughts
on how to balance learning and

00:36:48.742 --> 00:36:52.280
transforming your leadership style with
staying authentic at the same time?

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:53.135
&gt;&gt; Staying?

00:36:53.135 --> 00:36:54.687
&gt;&gt; Authentic.

00:36:54.687 --> 00:36:55.626
Authentic
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:55.626 --> 00:36:59.194
&gt;&gt; And transforming and
learning as you go [CROSSTALK]

00:36:59.194 --> 00:37:00.494
&gt;&gt; See I think we're growing,

00:37:00.494 --> 00:37:02.260
we're blooming, we're a planet.

00:37:02.260 --> 00:37:06.941
If I'm from India or I'm from China or
I'm from Minnesota and

00:37:06.941 --> 00:37:10.375
I deny who I am,
then you're not authentic.

00:37:10.375 --> 00:37:12.130
We grow from that point of view.

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:14.650
You grow from your strengths and
great leaders there,

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:17.662
I just had a program with
Malcolm Gladwell and Tom Banshee here but

00:37:17.662 --> 00:37:21.169
the whole idea of building on your
strengths and managing your weaknesses,

00:37:21.169 --> 00:37:25.220
but you've got to build on what makes you
really good and you may have weaknesses.

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:28.020
You want to have people around you
who can supplement those weakness.

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:30.865
Say you're not great in finance you
want to have a great financial officer.

00:37:30.865 --> 00:37:34.327
I went to Medtronics and I can tell you
I didn't know anything about medicine or

00:37:34.327 --> 00:37:35.334
medical technology.

00:37:35.334 --> 00:37:38.500
I knew a lot about technology,
but not about Medtronic.

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:42.830
I went up and gowned up and saw 700
procedures the time I was with Medtronic.

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:45.200
I used to come out here
to Stanford hospitals and

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:48.355
work with doctors, some of the great
work that a guy like Tom Fogerty or

00:37:48.355 --> 00:37:51.405
some of the great cardiac surgeons here,
or cardiologists.

00:37:51.405 --> 00:37:52.755
That's where I learned the business.

00:37:52.755 --> 00:37:53.926
But I wasn't trying to sell anything.

00:37:53.926 --> 00:37:57.346
But I think you have to build on that
base and learn what you don't know and

00:37:57.346 --> 00:37:57.987
recognize.

00:37:57.987 --> 00:38:00.665
You know I learned from
seeing all those procedures?

00:38:00.665 --> 00:38:03.715
I learned what I didn't know and
how much I didn't know.

00:38:03.715 --> 00:38:07.584
And if you're Larry Summers,
former president of Harvard and

00:38:07.584 --> 00:38:09.993
Obama's failed at economic adviser.

00:38:09.993 --> 00:38:13.750
If you know everything about everything,
I guarantee you you're going to fail.

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:17.940
So it's important to recognize what you
don't know and then build around that and

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.160
manage your weaknesses,
not try to exploit them.

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:22.710
You manage your weaknesses by
having people around that can

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:23.470
complement, offset them.

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:25.480
Yes sir.

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:27.810
Or ma'am, I was looking at the line.

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:29.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It seems like for leaders,

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.790
there's often a tradeoff between
being likeable and being respected.

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:35.460
Do you agree that that is a trade-off,
and how do you manage that?

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:37.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is a classic
Machiavellian question, right?

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:39.270
Should I be liked or respected?

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:41.880
I'll tell you,
you have to make hard decisions.

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:43.730
Don't make decisions just on being liked,
okay?

00:38:45.030 --> 00:38:47.980
But I think you want to be likeable.

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:52.740
You want to be kind and
thoughtful to people and

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:54.600
that's how you get the most out of them.

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:55.830
But you have to make hard decisions.

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:58.970
Sometimes you have to terminate
people that have worked for

00:38:58.970 --> 00:39:00.552
your company for a long time.

00:39:00.552 --> 00:39:03.600
Sometimes you're doing them a favor,
because they're in the wrong job.

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.580
So you have to make those hard calls.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:10.110
And I think in the end, if you do that
with humanity, and caring, in the end,

00:39:10.110 --> 00:39:13.330
you will be respected and
you'll get the results.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:15.300
And so I think these are very tough.

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:18.810
If you lean too heavy to the being liked,
you'll avoid conflict.

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:21.250
And in the end you won't
have any introspect

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:23.700
because you didn't make the hard call.

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:27.010
So, business is about hard decisions and

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:30.340
there's no way you can be in business and
not have to make hard calls.

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:31.490
But if you duck them or

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:35.470
you start doing them for what makes you
look good, particularly to the media,

00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:38.670
that's the sure sign that you're
about ready to go down the drain.

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:40.170
And you see people get caught up a lot,

00:39:40.170 --> 00:39:42.780
Donald Trump type figure,
it's all about them.

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:45.750
And when that happens, sell the stock.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:48.610
Thank you for the question.

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:50.435
Yes, sir?

00:39:50.435 --> 00:39:53.185
&gt;&gt; Hi, you mentioned earlier in your
elections how you kind of lost touch

00:39:53.185 --> 00:39:55.903
with the people around you and
kind of building those relationships.

00:39:55.903 --> 00:40:00.109
So, when you were in a leadership
position at a large organization,

00:40:00.109 --> 00:40:05.037
how did you maintain that philosophy, even
though you may be so far away from kind

00:40:05.037 --> 00:40:09.410
of the, You know the people that were
the last three feet from the consumers.

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:10.870
&gt;&gt; Stay in touch with those people.

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:14.356
&gt;&gt; Or even kind of the lower part
of the people in your organization.

00:40:14.356 --> 00:40:17.141
&gt;&gt; I know.
The only way to do it is to stay in touch.

00:40:17.141 --> 00:40:19.433
I mean we all ate in
the same cafeteria together.

00:40:19.433 --> 00:40:22.070
I would go in to sit down at
a table of a group of people.

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:22.730
Be in touch.

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:23.665
Wander through the labs.

00:40:23.665 --> 00:40:26.162
Go out and work with your sales people and
drive up and down here.

00:40:26.162 --> 00:40:29.507
Go and see that customer out in
Northern California that hates you,

00:40:29.507 --> 00:40:31.630
be in touch with
the people doing the work.

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:35.050
We have way too much administration,
too many layers.

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:37.780
You should never have fewer than
about 15 people working for you.

00:40:37.780 --> 00:40:39.020
The idea of this layering,

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:42.660
we gotta get rid of all these
detailed layers of administration and

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:46.900
put the emphasis on people doing the work,
the innovators, the quality people.

00:40:46.900 --> 00:40:49.230
The people that know how
to serve a customer.

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:51.090
The people that can build
a business in a country.

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:53.970
Those are the people that, and so
yeah, it takes a lot of travel.

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:57.530
I travel all over the world, all the time,
because you gotta be in touch with people.

00:40:57.530 --> 00:40:59.670
And you're always talking
about the mission or values.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:02.730
You sound like a broken record, but
you never stop talking about this,

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:04.450
because that's where people resonate.

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:06.900
You get to their hearts, their passion.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:08.790
And I think that's the only
way you can do it.

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:12.610
If you get caught up in the head shed, and
you see companies are very hierarchical.

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:14.960
I love the campus setting for companies.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:17.620
We tried to do it at Metronic,
made a company out here have it.

00:41:17.620 --> 00:41:19.480
You go to New York,
you see them all in these skyscrapers.

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:22.020
They don't even talk to
the person one floor below them.

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:25.120
So they get too caught up in sitting
in a board room looking at numbers, and

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:26.890
they lose sight of
the essence of the business.

00:41:26.890 --> 00:41:30.710
I can tell you that people I know are down
in the trading floors in financing.

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:37.790
And Bob Ulrich, CEO of Target for 14
years, he visited two dozen stores a week.

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:39.830
Howard Schultz did the same
thing at Starbucks.

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:41.340
You gotta be out there with the troops.

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:44.930
I think that's the only way you
can really know the business.

00:41:44.930 --> 00:41:48.490
Because if you lose sight of that, that's
why Howard recognized Starbucks was losing

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:51.040
it and
he went back into full-time leadership.

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:54.380
because he saw they were getting too
bureaucratic and getting away from it.

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:56.140
And the company was going down the drain.

00:41:56.140 --> 00:41:57.360
So thank you for the question.

00:41:58.950 --> 00:42:00.620
Yes?
&gt;&gt; Thanks a lot for your talk.

00:42:00.620 --> 00:42:04.020
As we look out, and most of us
are picking our careers for the future.

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.640
How do you think or
what do you think about balancing,

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:09.040
picking an organization where you
have a really strong mission?

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:12.887
And really strong alignment with
their mission vision versus picking

00:42:12.887 --> 00:42:17.138
an organization where you might get
better training or something like that?

00:42:17.138 --> 00:42:19.320
&gt;&gt; Depends on what kind of
training you're going to get.

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:20.640
You've have pretty good training here.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:23.150
You know I'm serious.

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:25.720
I mean you're not 18 years old.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:28.600
You've had pretty good training and
I think you want to look for

00:42:28.600 --> 00:42:30.626
an organization to give
you a chance to go do it.

00:42:30.626 --> 00:42:31.760
Throw you out there.

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:36.350
In business my son is up at
UCSF I was with him this weekend.

00:42:36.350 --> 00:42:38.920
He's in his mid 30s and
he's still a resident,

00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:40.590
a fourth year resident in head and
neck surgery.

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:44.188
He's training under one of the great
head and neck surgeons of the world.

00:42:44.188 --> 00:42:45.470
But medicine is different.

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:46.370
Business isn't like that.

00:42:46.370 --> 00:42:49.800
Best thing that can happen to you is
somebody bets on you and says, okay,

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:53.670
we want you to go run Pakistan for us,
or we want you to go run, you know,

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:55.070
we want you to go to run Austria.

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:58.320
But just having the experience of
being away and getting that leadership

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:02.590
experience, and because I think that's
where you learn about yourself and

00:43:02.590 --> 00:43:05.030
you really have to test
yourself against that.

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:08.680
And I think that's what really matters and
how you build yourself.

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:10.480
So training programs sound good.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:11.420
I'm not high on them.

00:43:11.420 --> 00:43:15.340
I have to tell you, I think most of them
are acculturation, trying to get you

00:43:15.340 --> 00:43:19.570
into the GE culture so you'll be a part of
that until you can be a great GE leader.

00:43:19.570 --> 00:43:21.960
Except you may not be
the leader you want to be and

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:23.590
you may not spend the rest
of your life there anyway.

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:26.940
And that's why a guy like
leaves GE very successful and

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:30.900
he fails Home Depot because he's
trying to apply all that GE stuff.

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:34.350
I'm not against GE, I'm just saying
I think the model is flawed.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:35.890
The model should be,

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:39.150
any leadership development program
should be about who you want to become.

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:42.600
And people that do that well,
like a General Mills, do it really well,

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:43.920
because they are focused on that.

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:45.330
So, thank you.

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:46.430
Yes, sir.

00:43:46.430 --> 00:43:47.170
&gt;&gt; Thank you for your time.

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:52.724
In light of the fact that
Steve Jobs denied his daughter and.

00:43:54.331 --> 00:43:57.310
Took credit for other people's ideas.

00:43:57.310 --> 00:44:00.540
Do you believe that he was
an authentic moral leader?

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:02.740
&gt;&gt; Steve Jobs,

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:06.930
it's really risky now we're calling
him the greatest leader of all time.

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:09.490
because he like the rest
of us had a lot of flaws.

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:12.140
I think he perhaps was the greatest
innovator of the last,

00:44:12.140 --> 00:44:14.810
he was in my opinion the greatest
innovator in the last 50 years.

00:44:14.810 --> 00:44:15.983
He did amazing things.

00:44:15.983 --> 00:44:17.392
But he learned the hard way,

00:44:17.392 --> 00:44:21.332
if you listen to his Stanford
commencement address in 2005, everything,

00:44:21.332 --> 00:44:24.652
he spent all this time working until
now this is all laid out there.

00:44:24.652 --> 00:44:27.051
He actually learned from his failures.

00:44:27.051 --> 00:44:30.026
He learned from failing and
getting fired at Apple.

00:44:30.026 --> 00:44:31.733
He learned what it was
like to be an orphan.

00:44:31.733 --> 00:44:34.815
Yes, he had difficult times,
yes he had parental issues.

00:44:34.815 --> 00:44:39.044
Yes he had family issues, because some of
those came out of his own family of origin

00:44:39.044 --> 00:44:40.840
into his family of choice.

00:44:40.840 --> 00:44:42.730
And so,
he had a lot of learning to do, but

00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:46.690
the good news is he processed and learned.

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:49.190
And he went off and created Pixar.

00:44:49.190 --> 00:44:51.460
He learned from those experiences and so

00:44:51.460 --> 00:44:56.610
when he came back to Apple and so called
Act 3, he was ready to do it much better.

00:44:56.610 --> 00:45:02.410
But he still could be a jerk, in fact, Ron
Johnson who created all the Apple stores,

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:04.360
came from Target, I knew him at Target.

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:07.690
He came to our CEO,
he's now the new CEO of JCPenny,

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:12.220
and he said Steve knew he
had these characteristics,

00:45:12.220 --> 00:45:17.280
these flaws and he lacked emotional
intelligence so he surrounded himself so

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:21.960
all the people around him at Apple had
high levels of emotional intelligence.

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:26.100
Johnny Ive and Tim cook and Ron Johnson
and that's the way he off set that so

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:27.249
that gets him the confidence.

00:45:27.249 --> 00:45:29.780
But I think he did it
really well in the end and

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:32.630
you can see in the results but
it took time.

00:45:32.630 --> 00:45:36.860
By the way he didn't spend any time
worrying about stock market or

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:40.160
getting stock price up it
took care of it's self.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:43.840
&gt;&gt; Yes
&gt;&gt; High, you spoke about empowerment, and

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:48.180
I just wanted to know how you empowered
people in an industry where there's so

00:45:48.180 --> 00:45:50.640
many procedures-
&gt;&gt; Yes, terrible.

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:51.905
&gt;&gt; And everything has to be
done by the book and all that.

00:45:51.905 --> 00:45:52.882
&gt;&gt; It's horrible.

00:45:52.882 --> 00:45:57.290
Every board I'm on is all these,
we're reviewing regulation and all.

00:45:57.290 --> 00:46:00.190
Now, I think, you have to do
all that stuff, so to speak.

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:01.040
You have to meet the FDA,

00:46:01.040 --> 00:46:03.740
you can't put drugs out that
don't meet the requirements.

00:46:03.740 --> 00:46:06.870
You have to comply with the regulations
and the financial service and

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:09.200
you have to comply with
the regulation in the energy industry.

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:11.170
You must do it,
you must have the discipline so

00:46:11.170 --> 00:46:15.432
you don't have a incident in the gulf like
BP did, you must do all those things.

00:46:15.432 --> 00:46:19.407
But I think really empower people finding
out what they're really passionate about,

00:46:19.407 --> 00:46:20.938
and giving them a chance to do it.

00:46:20.938 --> 00:46:23.151
Picking out people who want
to make a difference and

00:46:23.151 --> 00:46:24.548
putting them in jobs to go do it.

00:46:24.548 --> 00:46:26.655
Go start a venture,
you want to go do this?

00:46:26.655 --> 00:46:31.354
I met a young man that had,
born with a terrible face

00:46:31.354 --> 00:46:36.400
malformation, and
he actually had it cut in surgery.

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:39.521
Now he's taken up this feat
of pediatric oncology.

00:46:39.521 --> 00:46:43.010
To help people just like himself,
he's following that passion.

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:47.020
You know he shouldn't go off and
go to work for Meryl Lynch you know?

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.700
And I'm just saying you want to

00:46:49.700 --> 00:46:51.775
follow that line of what you
can do to make a difference.

00:46:51.775 --> 00:46:53.015
So, thank you.

00:46:53.015 --> 00:46:53.765
Yes sir.

00:46:53.765 --> 00:46:57.675
&gt;&gt; I was very interested on how you spoke
of aligning public and private interests.

00:46:57.675 --> 00:47:03.085
There's a new corporate legislation
passed on B corporations in California,

00:47:03.085 --> 00:47:07.280
Vermont, Maryland, about using the power
of business to create public benefit

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:11.540
without getting sued by shareholders for
not making profit their primary motive.

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:14.580
Do you think this is a trend that will
expand to the rest of the states?

00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:17.830
&gt;&gt; Yes, I refer to Porter's article,
that's what Porter says.

00:47:17.830 --> 00:47:21.800
Business has been fighting society for
the last 30 years, we need to get aligned.

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:23.530
We're chartered by society.

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:25.380
Society gives us our charter.

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:27.870
We should,
that should be the primary thing,

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:29.450
is how do we create value for society?

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:31.270
And then we create value.

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:33.620
So if you're an energy company,
you have to create value for society.

00:47:33.620 --> 00:47:36.310
If you're a healthcare company,
if you're a financial services company

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:39.490
your job is to finance other
people to start up companies.

00:47:39.490 --> 00:47:41.020
I was over at Center Hill
a few minutes ago,

00:47:41.020 --> 00:47:44.470
they're helping people get started
with total greenfield ventures.

00:47:44.470 --> 00:47:46.340
So we have that obligation.

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:48.920
And then from that,
we earn shareholder value.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:49.840
It's not the other way around.

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:52.690
If you start with the shareholder value,
you wind up putting yourself out of

00:47:52.690 --> 00:47:55.110
business, and
you'll eventually be bankrupt.

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:56.950
But I think it's a very good thing.

00:47:56.950 --> 00:48:00.160
And I thought also we're going to elude
to, I think one of the things that's

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:04.370
happening now is Social enterprises that
are nonprofit or for profit are also

00:48:04.370 --> 00:48:07.930
coming together much closer so that people
really get that in the nonprofit world.

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:10.650
But I think that those are also
coming much closer together.

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:13.620
I think you see many of the emerging
companies really understand

00:48:13.620 --> 00:48:17.030
what they're about and how they can and
we need to create the demand.

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:19.740
I think this idea of carving up the pie,
I hate

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:22.570
what's going on in the income disparity
in the country, I just can't stand it.

00:48:22.570 --> 00:48:26.190
But there's only one way around it,
we gotta expand the size of the pie

00:48:26.190 --> 00:48:28.970
by giving more people opportunities,
more people create companies,

00:48:28.970 --> 00:48:31.680
more people get financing,
get things started and get it going.

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:34.870
That's the future of this country,
and probably of the world.

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:35.800
Yes sir.

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:38.360
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for
a wonderful lecture.

00:48:38.360 --> 00:48:40.970
My question is a little bit philosophical,

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:45.140
I think I completely agree
with you to be authentic.

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:47.640
But my question is when
you are looking for

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:51.830
a job and
you find you have lots of interests and

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:55.950
actually you are sent here with A, I was
sent here with B, I was sent here with C.

00:48:55.950 --> 00:49:01.230
You find that you are sent here with a lot
of things, you have a lot of interests but

00:49:01.230 --> 00:49:05.760
finally, you should choose just one,
how to manage it?

00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:09.800
&gt;&gt; I think that's a discernment, that's a
reflection and introspection, think about

00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:13.440
where it is you want to devote yourself
and where can you make a difference?

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:15.190
And where are the opportunities?

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:17.400
Somebody's way maybe blocked over here?

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:20.190
I think that's part of why you're here.

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:25.070
This is an opportunity for a transforming
experience, and I hope that ten years from

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:27.800
now, you'll look back on this and
say this was a transforming experience.

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:29.610
It's an opportunity to
really think about that.

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:31.210
Don't take the highest paying job offer.

00:49:31.210 --> 00:49:33.910
We just had my 40th reunion
at Harvard Business School.

00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:37.400
I can tell you the people that took
the lowest paying jobs are the ones,

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:40.200
in the end, that did the best financially.

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:41.880
The highest paying kind of flattened out.

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:46.820
I think you want to focus on,
use this as an opportunity to really

00:49:46.820 --> 00:49:49.630
think through what do you want to do and
you may not hit it at first.

00:49:49.630 --> 00:49:52.460
Look at me, it took me forever to
get to the right place for me.

00:49:55.270 --> 00:49:58.410
There's no right place for all of us, but
there may be a right place for you, but

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:00.080
it may take you a while to get there.

00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:04.380
I always used to say to people,
if you hate your work, quit.

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:10.108
Don't spend your whole life going
through life sleepwalking through life,

00:50:10.108 --> 00:50:12.768
living somebody else's desires.

00:50:12.768 --> 00:50:14.521
Thank you.

00:50:14.521 --> 00:50:22.330
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:50:22.330 --> 00:50:23.320
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:50:23.320 --> 00:50:25.010
Can I just, one minute.

00:50:25.010 --> 00:50:25.830
Can I have one minute?

00:50:25.830 --> 00:50:29.160
I wanted to go back and
quote you something, the best advice.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:30.050
You mentioned Steve Jobs.

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:31.210
The best advice he ever gave me,

00:50:31.210 --> 00:50:32.992
it actually shows a high level
of emotional intelligence.

00:50:32.992 --> 00:50:37.840
He said and this is really the essence
of everything I've been trying to say,

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:41.250
don't spend your whole life
living somebody else's dream.

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:45.530
That's what I was doing with my father,
I had to be my dream and

00:50:45.530 --> 00:50:51.040
not his and I didn't even realize
that it was so inculcated in me.

00:50:52.310 --> 00:50:53.150
The second thing is,

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:57.810
don't let the noise of other people's
opinions drown out your inner voice.

00:50:57.810 --> 00:51:00.690
Listen to your inner voice,
that's the question you're asking.

00:51:00.690 --> 00:51:04.750
Don't let other people tell you what to
do, you gotta decide what you want to do.

00:51:04.750 --> 00:51:08.730
And third, most important of all,
follow your heart and your intuition.

00:51:09.845 --> 00:51:15.304
If you do that,
you'll have a very full life and

00:51:15.304 --> 00:51:21.315
you'll really make a big
difference in the world.

00:51:21.315 --> 00:51:22.250
Thank you.
[APPLAUSE]

